# CS 3410 Software Engineering  
## Assignment 2 — Architecture and Quality Plan for the Campus Library Reservation System

## 1. Context and Scope

The Campus Library Reservation System (CLRS) will replace the existing study-room reservation tool used across three university libraries. Students and staff will search room availability, create and manage reservations, receive reminders, and view schedules through room kiosks. Library staff will manage rooms, opening hours and recurring block bookings, and export monthly utilisation reports.

CLRS must integrate with the university’s SAML identity provider, facilities iCal feeds and a read-only PostgreSQL room-inventory database. The most significant business rule is that a user may reserve no more than three hours per day. Staff block bookings may override student reservations when at least 48 hours’ notice is provided.

This design covers the first release’s architecture, quality strategy, delivery process, operational monitoring and principal risks. It does not specify detailed user-interface designs or implement the system.

The proposed system uses hexagonal architecture with a framework-independent reservation domain at its centre. It will initially be deployed as a modular monolith to keep operation practical for a two-person team. External systems and infrastructure are accessed through ports and replaceable adapters.

The design prioritises:

* 99.5% availability during opening hours;
* availability-search p95 latency below 400 ms under 2,000 concurrent users;
* SAML authentication and auditable staff actions;
* configuration-only onboarding of additional libraries;
* database- and network-independent testing of reservation rules; and
* deployment and rollback within 10 minutes.

---

## 2. Architectural Decision

### 2.1 Decision

CLRS will use **hexagonal architecture**, also known as ports-and-adapters architecture, as its primary architectural style. The first release will be a **modular monolith** with one deployable application and separately deployable kiosk clients.

The central domain contains entities, value objects and use cases such as:

* `SearchAvailability`;
* `CreateReservation`;
* `ModifyReservation`;
* `CancelReservation`;
* `CreateBlockBooking`;
* `ApplyBlockBookingOverride`; and
* `CalculateUtilisation`.

The domain core has no dependency on HTTP frameworks, databases, SAML libraries, email providers or iCal implementations. It communicates with the outside world through interfaces.

#### Inbound ports

Inbound ports represent application use cases:

* `RoomSearchUseCase`;
* `ReservationUseCase`;
* `BlockBookingUseCase`;
* `KioskScheduleUseCase`;
* `ReminderUseCase`; and
* `UtilisationReportUseCase`.

HTTP controllers, background workers and kiosk endpoints call these ports.

#### Outbound ports

Outbound ports abstract external capabilities:

* `ReservationRepository`;
* `RoomInventoryPort`;
* `FacilitiesCalendarPort`;
* `IdentityPort`;
* `NotificationPort`;
* `KioskUpdatePort`;
* `AuditLogPort`;
* `Clock`; and
* `UnitOfWork`.

Adapters implement these interfaces. For example, a PostgreSQL adapter implements `ReservationRepository`, a SAML adapter implements `IdentityPort`, and an email/push adapter implements `NotificationPort`.

### 2.2 Rationale Against Quality Requirements

#### QR-1: Availability

The modular deployment reduces operational failure points compared with multiple independently deployed services. A health-checked application can be deployed behind two instances and a load balancer. PostgreSQL will use automated backups and high availability appropriate to the university environment.

Kiosks will maintain a local cache containing the last successfully retrieved schedule, its timestamp and a freshness indicator. If the CLRS endpoint or network becomes unavailable, the kiosk will continue displaying the last-known schedule and visibly indicate that it is offline. This provides graceful degradation rather than an empty or misleading display.

Reservation changes publish a `ReservationChanged` message through an outbound port after the transaction commits. A notification worker sends an update to the affected kiosk channel. Kiosks also poll periodically as a recovery mechanism. This avoids making reservation writes dependent on kiosk availability.

#### QR-2: Performance

The domain’s search use case is isolated from the HTTP and persistence mechanisms, allowing performance tests to target the complete search path independently. Availability searches will use indexed room attributes and time-range queries. Frequently requested room metadata will be cached because the legacy inventory database is read-only and should not be queried for every request.

Search results may be cached for a short period, such as 10–30 seconds, while reservation creation always performs an authoritative conflict check in the reservation database. This deliberately separates fast discovery from authoritative booking.

The system will be load-tested at 2,000 concurrent users, with a release gate requiring p95 search latency below 400 ms and an agreed error-rate threshold.

#### QR-3: Security

Authentication is isolated behind the `IdentityPort`. The SAML adapter validates assertions and maps the university identity to a local user and role. The domain receives an authenticated principal rather than SAML-specific objects.

Authorisation is enforced both at the application boundary and within use cases. For example, only staff users may create block bookings or export reports. Staff actions produce audit records containing actor identifier, action, target and timestamp. Normal application logs contain correlation IDs, status codes, timings and technical error information, but no names, email addresses, SAML assertions or reservation descriptions containing PII.

#### QR-4: Modifiability

Library-specific details are represented as configuration:

* campus and library identifiers;
* opening hours and holidays;
* room capabilities;
* kiosk-to-room mappings;
* facilities calendar URLs; and
* report configuration.

The domain operates on generic `Campus`, `Room`, `OpeningHours` and `Equipment` concepts. Adding a library therefore requires data/configuration changes, validation and deployment, rather than a new code path. Adapters isolate differences in external calendar or inventory formats.

#### QR-5: Testability

The reservation rules are implemented in pure domain services and value objects. They accept explicit inputs such as user, room, requested interval, existing reservations, opening hours and clock time. They return domain results or typed errors.

Consequently, tests for the three-hour limit, overlapping reservations, modifications, cancellations, recurring blocks and 48-hour notice do not require a database, network or SAML provider. Fake implementations of outbound ports can be injected into application-service tests. This is a direct benefit of hexagonal architecture.

#### QR-6: Operability

A modular monolith is simpler for a two-person team to deploy, monitor and troubleshoot than several microservices. The application will be packaged as an immutable container. Database migrations will be backward-compatible where possible, and deployment will use a rolling or blue-green approach. The previous container image remains available for rollback.

A tested rollback procedure must restore application traffic to the previous version within 10 minutes. Feature flags will allow risky features such as kiosk push updates or new report formats to be disabled without redeployment.

### 2.3 Rejected Alternatives and Trade-offs

#### Layered monolith

A layered monolith would also be easy to deploy and could satisfy the availability and operational requirements. However, traditional layers often allow business logic to become coupled to controllers, ORM models or framework services. That would weaken QR-5 over time. Hexagonal architecture provides stronger dependency direction and makes external integrations replaceable.

The cost is additional interfaces, adapters and dependency-injection configuration. For a small application, this is more design effort than a simple layered implementation.

#### Microservices

Microservices could independently scale search, notifications and reporting. They could also isolate failures, for example preventing a report export from consuming reservation resources. However, the proposed system has a small operational team and only three libraries. Microservices would introduce service discovery, distributed tracing, API versioning, multiple deployment pipelines and cross-service failure modes.

They would make QR-6 more difficult and could reduce QR-1 if a reservation operation depends on several unavailable services. Distributed transactions would also complicate conflict prevention and notification consistency. The modular monolith preserves internal boundaries while avoiding premature distribution. The modules can be extracted later if measured load or organisational growth justifies it.

#### Event-driven architecture with CQRS

CQRS would provide excellent scalability for search and kiosk read models. An event log could also support audit and reporting. However, event-driven processing introduces eventual consistency. A user might search an apparently free room immediately after another user reserves it, or a kiosk might temporarily show an outdated schedule. These issues are particularly sensitive for QR-1 and reservation conflict handling.

CQRS also requires event schema evolution, replay procedures, projection monitoring and more complex recovery. The first release does not need that level of infrastructure. The design does use a limited transactional outbox and asynchronous notifications, gaining reliable kiosk and reminder updates without making event-driven CQRS the primary style.

---

## 3. Structure

### 3.1 Container/Component Diagram

```text
+-------------------+       HTTPS/SSE or polling       +----------------------+
| Student/staff web | -------------------------------> |                      |
+-------------------+                                  |   CLRS Application   |
                                                       |                      |
+-------------------+       HTTPS                      |  HTTP/API adapters   |
| Room kiosk clients| -------------------------------> |  SAML adapter        |
+-------------------+                                  |  Application services|
                                                       |  Domain core         |
+-------------------+                                  |  Outbound adapters    |
| Staff/report user | -------------------------------> |  Background workers   |
+-------------------+                                  +----------+-----------+
                                                                  |
                  +----------------------+-------------------------+------------------+
                  |                      |                         |                  |
          +-------v-------+      +-------v--------+        +-------v------+   +-------v------+
          | CLRS          |      | Legacy room    |        | Facilities   |   | SAML identity |
          | PostgreSQL    |      | inventory DB   |        | iCal feeds   |   | provider      |
          | reservations, |      | read-only      |        |              |   |               |
          | outbox, audit|      +----------------+        +--------------+   +--------------+
          +-------+-------+
                  |
          +-------v--------+       +----------------------+
          | Notification   |       | Email/push provider  |
          | worker         |------>|                      |
          +----------------+       +----------------------+

          +----------------+
          | Metrics/logging|
          | and alerting   |
          +----------------+
```

The application is internally divided into domain, application, adapter and infrastructure modules. Dependencies point inward toward the domain. The domain does not import database or web-framework packages.

The database contains reservations, block bookings, campus configuration, audit records and an outbox table. A database constraint or serialised transaction prevents overlapping reservations for the same room. The outbox ensures that a committed reservation change is not lost before kiosk and reminder processing.

### 3.2 FR-1 Walkthrough: Search Availability

1. The user authenticates through the SAML adapter.
2. The HTTP adapter validates query parameters such as campus, capacity, equipment, start time and end time.
3. The controller invokes `SearchAvailability` through the inbound port.
4. The application service obtains room metadata through `RoomInventoryPort` and opening-hours information through configuration.
5. It queries reservations and block bookings through `ReservationRepository`.
6. The domain service removes rooms that are closed, lack requested equipment or have an overlapping reservation/block.
7. The response adapter converts the domain result to JSON.

Room metadata is cached with a controlled expiry. Reservation availability is checked against the authoritative reservation database. Invalid time windows and unauthorised campus access result in typed errors rather than database exceptions being exposed to clients.

### 3.3 FR-4 Walkthrough: Kiosk Updates

1. A reservation, modification, cancellation or block booking is committed through the reservation use case.
2. The transaction writes the reservation change and an outbox record for the affected room.
3. A worker reads the outbox and publishes a room-specific schedule update through `KioskUpdatePort`.
4. Connected kiosks receive the update through server-sent events. Kiosks without a persistent connection poll the kiosk schedule endpoint.
5. The kiosk writes the received schedule and timestamp to local storage.
6. If no update is received for a configured period, the kiosk displays the cached schedule and an “offline/last updated” indicator.

The service records the time of the reservation commit and the time the kiosk acknowledges the update. The target is that at least 99% of updates arrive within 10 seconds.

---

## 4. Quality Plan

### 4.1 Testing Strategy

#### Unit tests

Unit tests cover the framework-independent domain:

* three-hour-per-day limit;
* overlapping reservations;
* modification and cancellation ownership;
* opening-hours validation;
* recurring block-booking expansion;
* 48-hour block-booking notice;
* block-booking precedence;
* role-based permissions;
* reminder timing; and
* time-zone and daylight-saving boundaries.

The `Clock` port allows deterministic tests. Property-based tests will generate intervals to identify overlap and boundary defects. These tests run on every commit and require no database or network, satisfying QR-5.

#### Application-service tests

Application tests use fake outbound ports and test orchestration, transaction handling and error mapping. Examples include:

* a failed repository write does not publish an outbox event;
* a successful reservation creates exactly one notification task;
* staff actions create an audit record;
* an unauthenticated or non-staff principal cannot create a block booking; and
* a legacy inventory failure produces the intended degraded search response.

#### Adapter and integration tests

Integration tests run against containerised PostgreSQL and verify:

* schema and migrations;
* unique and overlap constraints;
* transaction isolation;
* outbox processing;
* repository queries and indexes;
* SAML metadata and assertion mapping using a test identity provider;
* iCal parsing and malformed-feed handling; and
* email/push provider retry behaviour.

External providers are tested with sandbox accounts or contract simulators rather than production systems.

#### Contract tests

Contracts define:

* HTTP request and response schemas;
* error codes, including reservation conflict `409`;
* kiosk schedule format;
* notification-worker messages;
* SAML identity mapping assumptions; and
* iCal and legacy inventory adapter expectations.

Consumer-driven contracts will ensure that kiosk clients remain compatible with the API. Contract tests are especially important because adapters isolate external systems whose behaviour CLRS does not control.

#### End-to-end tests

A small suite will run in a staging environment:

1. authenticate through test SAML;
2. search for a room;
3. create a reservation;
4. verify a conflict for an overlapping request;
5. modify and cancel the reservation;
6. create a staff block booking;
7. verify kiosk schedule update;
8. verify reminder scheduling; and
9. export a utilisation report.

The suite will avoid excessive dependence on timing by using a controllable test clock where possible.

#### Performance, resilience and security tests

Load tests will simulate 2,000 concurrent users, with emphasis on search at semester-start traffic levels. The release gate is p95 below 400 ms, acceptable error rate and no database saturation.

Failure-injection tests will stop the iCal feed, notification provider, outbox worker and one application instance. Expected behaviour includes cached inventory, retried notifications, continued reservation processing and kiosk fallback.

Security tests will include dependency scanning, static analysis, authentication bypass attempts, authorisation tests, log-PII scanning and penetration testing of the public API.

### 4.2 Delivery

Development uses short-lived feature branches merged into `main` through pull requests. Each pull request requires:

* code review by the other team member where practical;
* formatting, static analysis and dependency checks;
* unit and application tests;
* integration tests;
* contract tests;
* database migration validation; and
* no detected PII in structured logs.

`main` must remain deployable. Releases are tagged and built into immutable container images. A staging deployment runs integration, end-to-end and smoke tests before production approval.

Production deployment uses a rolling or blue-green strategy:

1. apply backward-compatible database migrations;
2. deploy the new application image;
3. run health and smoke checks;
4. gradually shift traffic;
5. monitor latency, errors and reservation conflicts; and
6. complete or abort the rollout.

The previous image and configuration are retained. Rollback is performed by shifting traffic back to the previous image and disabling any new feature flags. A rehearsal must demonstrate rollback within 10 minutes. Database changes must be expand-and-contract so that the previous application version remains compatible during rollback.

### 4.3 Observability

#### Logs

Structured logs contain:

* timestamp;
* severity;
* correlation/request ID;
* route or use case;
* campus and room identifier where non-sensitive;
* outcome and error category;
* latency;
* deployment version; and
* dependency name and timing.

Logs must not contain names, email addresses, SAML assertions, access tokens, reservation notes or other PII. Audit records are separate, access-controlled and retained according to university policy.

#### Metrics

For QR-1:

* successful request rate;
* HTTP 5xx rate;
* application instance health;
* database connection pool usage;
* database availability;
* outbox backlog age;
* notification failure rate;
* kiosk update delivery latency;
* kiosk last-successful-sync age; and
* percentage of kiosks using cached schedules.

For QR-2:

* search request count;
* search p50, p95 and p99 latency;
* reservation-write latency;
* database query latency;
* cache hit ratio;
* CPU and memory;
* active connections; and
* timeout and rate-limit counts.

#### Alerts

Alerts include:

* monthly availability projection below 99.5%;
* five-minute 5xx rate above 2%;
* search p95 above 400 ms for 10 consecutive minutes;
* database availability or connection exhaustion;
* outbox records older than 30 seconds;
* more than 1% of kiosk updates exceeding 10 seconds over 15 minutes; and
* any campus with more than 5% of kiosks using schedules older than five minutes.

Dashboards will show these measures by campus and deployment version. Synthetic probes will periodically search availability and retrieve a kiosk schedule during opening hours.

